{"reason": "The execution results confirm the initial plan's diagnosis: validation log loss oscillates (0.39-0.53) and the model underfits due to using only 80% of data. The initial plan proposes full-data retraining with low LR and CE-based checkpoint selection. Historical successful experiments show that attention-weighted pooling over last 4 hidden layers consistently improves log loss by ~0.01-0.02, while failed BiLSTM heads add complexity without benefit. Therefore, I enhance the initial plan by adding a model_design change: replacing mean pooling with attention-weighted pooling over last 4 hidden layers, which historically stabilizes validation and captures richer stylistic features. The training_evaluation changes from the initial plan are retained and refined: after loading best CE-loss checkpoint, perform 3 epochs of full-data training with LR 5e-6 (historical successful low-LR fine-tuning), and use cross-entropy loss (not log-loss) for checkpoint selection to avoid probability sharpening artifacts. These modifications align with the initial plan's direction (post-training improvement, better checkpointing) and leverage historical insights to boost performance without overcomplicating the architecture.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Modify SpookyAuthorClassifier.forward() to extract hidden_states from all layers (outputs.hidden_states), take the last 4 layers, apply mean pooling (masked) to each, then compute a weighted sum using a learnable 4-element weight vector (nn.Parameter initialized to 0.25) followed by softmax normalization. This replaces the single-layer CLS pooling. This change is historically successful (~0.01-0.02 log loss improvement) and captures multi-level stylistic features crucial for author identification. Keep backbone unfrozen last 8 layers, single linear head, dropout 0.3. Preserve forward signature (input_ids, attention_mask) and return logits.", "training_evaluation": "Implement two changes from the initial plan: (1) Change checkpoint selection criterion from validation log-loss to validation cross-entropy loss (the raw loss before softmax). Save best model as './working/best_model.pt'. (2) After loading the best checkpoint, perform 3 additional epochs of training on the full training set (train+validation combined) with a fixed learning rate of 5e-6. Use the same optimizer configuration but only one param group (all parameters trained at 5e-6) for this final phase. Then use this refitted model for test inference. This addresses underfitting by exposing the model to 100% of data with a low LR to prevent catastrophic forgetting, inspired by historical successful low-LR fine-tuning experiments. Keep all other training hyperparameters unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm the initial plan's diagnosis: validation log loss oscillates (0.39-0.53) and the model underfits due to using only 80% of data. The initial plan proposes full-data retraining with low LR and CE-based checkpoint selection. Historical successful experiments show that attention-weighted pooling over last 4 hidden layers consistently improves log loss by ~0.01-0.02, while failed BiLSTM heads add complexity without benefit. Therefore, I enhance the initial plan by adding a model_design change: replacing mean pooling with attention-weighted pooling over last 4 hidden layers, which historically stabilizes validation and captures richer stylistic features. The training_evaluation changes from the initial plan are retained and refined: after loading best CE-loss checkpoint, perform 3 epochs of full-data training with LR 5e-6 (historical successful low-LR fine-tuning), and use cross-entropy loss (not log-loss) for checkpoint selection to avoid probability sharpening artifacts. These modifications align with the initial plan's direction (post-training improvement, better checkpointing) and leverage historical insights to boost performance without overcomplicating the architecture.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Modify SpookyAuthorClassifier.forward() to extract hidden_states from all layers (outputs.hidden_states), take the last 4 layers, apply mean pooling (masked) to each, then compute a weighted sum using a learnable 4-element weight vector (nn.Parameter initialized to 0.25) followed by softmax normalization. This replaces the single-layer CLS pooling. This change is historically successful (~0.01-0.02 log loss improvement) and captures multi-level stylistic features crucial for author identification. Keep backbone unfrozen last 8 layers, single linear head, dropout 0.3. Preserve forward signature (input_ids, attention_mask) and return logits.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement two changes from the initial plan: (1) Change checkpoint selection criterion from validation log-loss to validation cross-entropy loss (the raw loss before softmax). Save best model as './working/best_model.pt'. (2) After loading the best checkpoint, perform 3 additional epochs of training on the full training set (train+validation combined) with a fixed learning rate of 5e-6. Use the same optimizer configuration but only one param group (all parameters trained at 5e-6) for this final phase. Then use this refitted model for test inference. This addresses underfitting by exposing the model to 100% of data with a low LR to prevent catastrophic forgetting, inspired by historical successful low-LR fine-tuning experiments. Keep all other training hyperparameters unchanged."
