}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting: training loss drops from 0.9213 to 0.4260 while validation log loss oscillates (best 0.3841) and final full-data retraining (LR fixed 5e-6) further reduces training loss without improving validation. Historical experiments show that (1) proper linear warmup + cosine annealing without restarts (successful experiment 1) stabilizes LR and prevents premature zero LR, and (2) attention-weighted pooling over last 4 layers (successful experiments) consistently improves generalization by ~0.01-0.02 log loss. The initial plan's stochastic depth can be simplified to this proven pooling approach. Failed BiLSTM experiments confirm complex heads hurt performance. The most promising modifications are: (1) model_design: replace the learnable layer_weights with a small attention module over the last 4 hidden layers to capture non-linear importance, and (2) training_evaluation: fix the broken warmup (compounding with scheduler) by using a SequentialLR (Linear warmup + CosineAnnealingLR without restarts) and remove the obsolete full-data retraining (which overfits). This directly addresses the observed validation loss oscillation and overfitting.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current learnable layer_weights (nn.Parameter with softmax) with a lightweight attention pooling module. After extracting the last 4 hidden states and applying masked mean pooling, stack them into a (batch, 4, hidden) tensor, then pass through a single linear layer (with hidden size) to compute scalar scores per layer (no non-linearity), apply softmax over layers, and compute weighted sum. This follows successful historical experiments where attention pooling improved log loss by enabling per-sample non-linear layer importance. Keep the backbone unfrozen last 8 layers and linear head unchanged. Ensure forward signature (input_ids, attention_mask) returns logits.", "training_evaluation": "Fix the learning rate schedule by replacing the current buggy warmup (compounding manual scaling with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts) with a proper SequentialLR: LinearLR (start_factor=0.1, total_iters=warmup_steps) followed by CosineAnnealingLR (T_max=total_steps-warmup_steps, eta_min=1e-6). Remove manual warmup in loop; call scheduler.step() once per batch after optimizer.step(). Also remove the separate full-data retraining phase (3 epochs low LR) as it overfits; instead, train for 15 epochs with the new scheduler and early stopping (patience=5) on validation log loss. Keep other hyperparameters identical."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting: training loss drops from 0.9213 to 0.4260 while validation log loss oscillates (best 0.3841) and final full-data retraining (LR fixed 5e-6) further reduces training loss without improving validation. Historical experiments show that (1) proper linear warmup + cosine annealing without restarts (successful experiment 1) stabilizes LR and prevents premature zero LR, and (2) attention-weighted pooling over last 4 layers (successful experiments) consistently improves generalization by ~0.01-0.02 log loss. The initial plan's stochastic depth can be simplified to this proven pooling approach. Failed BiLSTM experiments confirm complex heads hurt performance. The most promising modifications are: (1) model_design: replace the learnable layer_weights with a small attention module over the last 4 hidden layers to capture non-linear importance, and (2) training_evaluation: fix the broken warmup (compounding with scheduler) by using a SequentialLR (Linear warmup + CosineAnnealingLR without restarts) and remove the obsolete full-data retraining (which overfits). This directly addresses the observed validation loss oscillation and overfitting.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current learnable layer_weights (nn.Parameter with softmax) with a lightweight attention pooling module. After extracting the last 4 hidden states and applying masked mean pooling, stack them into a (batch, 4, hidden) tensor, then pass through a single linear layer (with hidden size) to compute scalar scores per layer (no non-linearity), apply softmax over layers, and compute weighted sum. This follows successful historical experiments where attention pooling improved log loss by enabling per-sample non-linear layer importance. Keep the backbone unfrozen last 8 layers and linear head unchanged. Ensure forward signature (input_ids, attention_mask) returns logits.",
    "training_evaluation": "Fix the learning rate schedule by replacing the current buggy warmup (compounding manual scaling with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts) with a proper SequentialLR: LinearLR (start_factor=0.1, total_iters=warmup_steps) followed by CosineAnnealingLR (T_max=total_steps-warmup_steps, eta_min=1e-6). Remove manual warmup in loop; call scheduler.step() once per batch after optimizer.step(). Also remove the separate full-data retraining phase (3 epochs low LR) as it overfits; instead, train for 15 epochs with the new scheduler and early stopping (patience=5) on validation log loss. Keep other hyperparameters identical."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The validation log loss plateaued around 0.384-0.434 with spikes, while training loss continued decreasing, indicating underutilization of model capacity and overfitting. The initial plan's core direction (richer representation, deeper unfreezing, and focused loss) is supported by successful historical experiments that used multi-layer attention pooling and proper warmup/cosine annealing (achieving ~0.01-0.02 log loss improvement), and failed experiments (BiLSTM heads, OneCycleLR with warm restarts) that added complexity without benefit. Therefore, I select model_design to implement multi-layer attention pooling (proven successful), training_evaluation to fix the scheduler and unfreeze more layers with progressive unfreezing (matching historical successes), and data_processing_and_feature_engineering to add simple text length and punctuation features (a light addition that aligns with the initial plan's spirit of enrichment without overcomplicating).", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add two lightweight features: sentence length (number of characters) and punctuation count (number of '.' ',' '!' '?' ';' ':'), computed on the raw text and concatenated to the pooled representation before the classification head. This adds minimal parameters but provides direct stylistic signals (e.g., Lovecraft uses longer sentences, Poe uses more dashes). Implement by modifying the forward method to accept an additional tensor of shape (batch, 2) and append it to the pooled hidden state before the final linear layer. The tokenizer and dataset remain unchanged except for extracting these features in the dataset __getitem__ method.", "model_design": "Replace the current single-learnable-weight attention pooling over last 4 layers with a multi-head attention pooling mechanism: use nn.MultiheadAttention (8 heads, hidden_size/8 per head) where the query is a learned tensor of shape (1, hidden_size), keys and values are the mean-pooled representations from the last 4 layers (stacked as (4, batch, hidden_size)). The output is the weighted combination of layers, followed by a residual connection with the global mean pool of the last layer. This directly implements the initial plan's 'multi-head cross-attention layer' upgrade, informed by historical successes that showed attention-weighted multi-layer pooling improves log loss by 0.01-0.02. The head definition remains unchanged (single linear layer). Preserve forward interface.", "training_evaluation": "Implement progressive unfreezing: freeze all backbone layers initially; after 2 epochs, unfreeze the last 8 layers; after 4 epochs, unfreeze the last 12 layers; after 6 epochs, unfreeze the last 16 layers. Use a proper scheduler: linear warmup for 10% of total steps (start_factor=0.1) followed by cosine annealing with eta_min=1e-6 (same as current but with clear milestones). Increase epochs to 20 and patience to 7. Additionally, replace cross-entropy loss with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0) and add class weights [0.42, 0.31, 0.27] to address hard example underperformance. This combines successful historical scheduler usage with the initial plan's focal loss suggestion."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The validation log loss plateaued around 0.384-0.434 with spikes, while training loss continued decreasing, indicating underutilization of model capacity and overfitting. The initial plan's core direction (richer representation, deeper unfreezing, and focused loss) is supported by successful historical experiments that used multi-layer attention pooling and proper warmup/cosine annealing (achieving ~0.01-0.02 log loss improvement), and failed experiments (BiLSTM heads, OneCycleLR with warm restarts) that added complexity without benefit. Therefore, I select model_design to implement multi-layer attention pooling (proven successful), training_evaluation to fix the scheduler and unfreeze more layers with progressive unfreezing (matching historical successes), and data_processing_and_feature_engineering to add simple text length and punctuation features (a light addition that aligns with the initial plan's spirit of enrichment without overcomplicating).",
